WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
Like many of you, you probably first heard about Bitcoin

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.880
around the time that it first appeared,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.920
in 2008 was when Satoshi's first paper came out.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.280
And I think like many of you at that point I was a little

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.700
concerned that this internet thing that we had built

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.440
on top of was recentralizing in a way.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.153
I mean it was never really totally decentralized

00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:23.980
and we can have an interesting conversation during QA

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.840
about the word "decentralization."

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.320
But it was certainly a place that felt flatter.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.040
It was a place where you could run your own mail server.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.700
You could run your own website.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.200
You could run a lot of your own software.
You didn't have to,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.380
but the fact that you could

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.000
was an incredibly liberating kind of thing.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.220
There was just enough decentralization on the internet

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:47.900
that it was a place where
a lot of new businesses could be formed.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.520
And I don't know if this makes me a good ancestor

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.280
or not but I along with some others here

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.700
were complicit in putting the first ad banner on the web.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.480
And I tell people I've been atoning for it ever since.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.300
But even in those days it felt like the internet was

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.060
this wide-open frontier

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.720
but by 2008 it didn't quite feel so much as like a frontier.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.100
It felt a little more like

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:15.000
things were organizing to get back to what
Thomas J Watson, the founder of IBM, said which

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.900
was maybe there's only a market for actually five of these computers in the world.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.340
It turned out that they would be:
Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, etc.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.060
So at that point the launch of Bitcoin

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.380
felt like like there were a number of projects

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.440
along these lines that were about

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.680
a set of developers going

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.100
"Can't we just reinvent the underlying protocols to not have this centralizing network effect?"

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.420
Is there a way that we can make it cheap again,

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.800
and create a financial incentive and a protocol

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.080
that made it so that you could host things in a decentralized way

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.600
and didn't have to worry about these 
centers of gravity forming out there in the world.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.220
Impossibly idealistic. I resonated with it, 
but I had some issues with it, too.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.500
I had some issues with the idea of proof-of-work

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.680
serving as the fundamental basis for how this works.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.980
And I can go into it in a bit, but proof-of-work is basically

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.260
burning CPU power to run a lottery to figure out
who puts the next link in a chain.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.940
Which for a lot of as they start looking at this

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.500
makes you go, ewww. I'm not sure I want to get into this.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.060
I kind of call it the mercury mining of our generation.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.860
In that it's cool technically,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:27.260
but also kind of icky once you get into it. 
And certainly can leave a toxic legacy.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.040
What I also was concerned about was

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.340
whether a speculative financial instrument

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.420
really was the right way to build the web 3.0, right?

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.060
Was there a risk that this would be gamed

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.520
the way financial markets have always been gamed?

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:45.880
And we would find a whole new set 
of empires being built out of thin air

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.300
fairly or unfairly.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.960
Because the internet never felt that way.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.960
Internet technologies and protocols 
you'd release a piece of software,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:57.080
the thousandth person to adopt it didn't benefit from it less than the first person to adopt it.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.020
In fact they probably benefited more it was 
a snowball effect that grew and grew.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.960
And so I stayed kind of away from Bitcoin
much to again our family's detriment.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.420
I did not invest early on in Bitcoin

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.700
or any of the others. But kept my eye on it.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.440
And it was when I kind of realized that 
there was perhaps another take on it

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.220
which was to look at the technology underneath

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:24.000
cryptocurrencies to try to understand was there something innovative and unique there.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.860
And a way to detach it from 
some of these things that felt weird

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.000
that I decided to jump in.

